---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyHourlySalary from '@/components/tools/finance/CalcMoneyHourlySalary.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="hourly-to-salary-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyHourlySalary client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Hourly to salary in one line</h2>
    <p>
      Annual salary = hourly rate × hours per week × paid weeks per year. At the standard 40 hours and
      52 weeks that is <strong>2,080 hours</strong>. Multiply an hourly rate by 2,080 for the annual
      salary, and divide an annual salary by 2,080 for the hourly rate.
    </p>
    <p>
      A mental shortcut: double the hourly rate and add three zeros. 25 an hour → 50,000 a year. It is
      exact at 2,000 hours and about 4% low at 2,080, which is close enough to check a job advert.
    </p>

    <h2>The mistake everyone makes with monthly pay</h2>
    <p>
      A month is not four weeks. It averages 4.348 weeks, so weekly pay × 4 is about 8% too low: about a
      month's pay a year. The monthly figure is always the annual salary divided by twelve, as above.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fortnightly pay has the same problem. Twenty-six fortnightly packets do not divide evenly into
      twelve months, so two months each year have three pay dates. Budget on the monthly average, not
      on the pay dates.
    </p>

    <h2>Semi-monthly and bi-weekly are not the same</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[460px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Schedule</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Packets a year</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">On a 52,000 salary</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>Weekly</td><td style="text-align:right">52</td><td style="text-align:right">1,000.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bi-weekly (every two weeks)</td><td style="text-align:right">26</td><td style="text-align:right">2,000.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Semi-monthly (twice a month)</td><td style="text-align:right">24</td><td style="text-align:right">2,166.67</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Monthly</td><td style="text-align:right">12</td><td style="text-align:right">4,333.33</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The annual total is the same. Only the cheque size and the timing differ. Confusing bi-weekly and
      semi-monthly is a common payroll error, and the 8.3% difference looks like a pay cut.
    </p>

    <h2>Paid holiday, unpaid time off, and the weeks field</h2>
    <p>
      Leave <strong>weeks per year at 52</strong> if your holiday is paid. A salaried employee gets pay
      for all fifty-two weeks, worked or not, so the hourly equivalent of a salary uses 2,080 hours for
      any amount of leave.
    </p>
    <p>
      So a salary with more paid leave is worth more per hour worked. To compare two offers, use the
      hours you will actually work. The unpaid-days field does this for contractors and hourly staff who
      get no pay for days off.
    </p>

    <h2>Comparing an hourly contract with a salaried job</h2>
    <p>
      The same rate does not make the same offer. A contractor at 60 an hour and an employee at 124,800
      look identical on this page, but they differ:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Unpaid time off: four weeks of holiday and ten public holidays is about 240 hours, or 12% of
        the year.
      </li>
      <li>
        Employer payroll taxes: a self-employed contractor usually pays both halves.
      </li>
      <li>
        Benefits: health cover, pension matching, sick pay, parental leave and insurance are all
        compensation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Utilisation: very few contractors bill 100% of available hours, and unbilled weeks are unpaid.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      As a rule of thumb, a contract rate must be 25–40% above the salaried hourly equivalent to be
      comparable. To model it here, reduce weeks per year and add the unpaid days you expect.
    </p>

    <h2>Overtime</h2>
    <p>
      Enter overtime hours separately. They are paid at a multiple of the base rate and added to the
      base year. So the base hourly rate stays correct: with 5 hours a week at time and a half, the
      annual total goes up but the base rate does not. Use the base rate to compare offers.
    </p>
    <p>
      Legal rules differ. US federal law (the FLSA) requires time and a half above 40 hours in a week
      for non-exempt employees. California adds a daily rule. Most of Europe sets overtime by contract
      and collective agreement. Set the multiplier to match your own arrangement.
    </p>

    <h2>Everything here is gross</h2>
    <p>
      The page applies no tax. Take-home pay depends on country, state or province, filing status,
      pension contributions, student loan plan and other details that this page does not ask for. Use
      these figures as the top line and use your local tax calculator for the rest.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing is transmitted</h2>
    <p>
      The arithmetic and the exports run in your browser, and the page also works offline.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
